Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Statement Four Returns at Half Speed

Story & Context: At 70 BPM, Milena begins with a dry spoken statement describing an unclaimed suitcase logged into storage under case number four. Her gusle holds a rough tonic beneath the sentence with small downward seconds at phrase endings. Darnell introduces only one kick every two beats and leaves the first sixteen bars almost empty. When Milena begins a new paragraph, the line “the key was still inside” returns from tape at half speed beneath her live narration. Jelena answers the end of the dragged phrase with one open Rhodes fifth. Andre contributes a single muted frame-drum hit after the tape stops. Luka enters with one low bass-clarinet fundamental while the sub remains absent. In the final section, the complete opening sentence returns unchanged but slowed only slightly, making it sound less ominous and more factual than the half-speed fragment.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Open municipal evidence ledger on entry 4 beside an old suitcase key, two horizontal speech waveforms of the exact same sentence printed at different lengths, gusle bow hair crossing the page edge. Lyric material should read like a concise inventory statement with one repeated line: “the key was still inside.” Avoid abstract ghost language.

Track 2: Room B Keeps Two Versions of Tuesday

Story & Context: At 76 BPM, the track uses the EP's clearest drum pulse: short kick, occasional low clap, no constant hats. Milena recites a sequence of events tied to a Tuesday afternoon in archive Room B. The first pass is entirely live. Eight bars later, Darnell reintroduces two words from the account as STALL fragments with long silence between repetitions. The live gusle never changes tempo, creating an audible split between narrative present and stretched memory. Jelena plays clipped suspended-second Rhodes chords after each stalled word but never beneath them. Andre replaces one programmed clap with a frame-drum rim stroke every fourth cycle. Luka uses breath-heavy minor-second falls during the largest speech gaps. The ending overlays the original dry phrase with a three-quarter-speed SHADOW copy, producing two versions of Tuesday without changing a single noun or verb.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Institutional door marked ROOM B, two paper calendars both showing Tuesday but with different clock times handwritten beneath them, cassette leader tape hanging beside a vertical gusle silhouette. Lyrics should remain procedural: room number, time, object position, who entered, who left. Repeated fragments limited to “Tuesday,” “Room B,” and one unchanged factual sentence.

Track 3: Nothing in the Transcript Was Corrected

Story & Context: The closer runs at 64 BPM and begins without drums or sub. Milena reads a short testimony about a transport log containing an error nobody amended. The gusle uses one sustained bowed pitch with pressure swells rather than melodic motion. Darnell waits almost two minutes before introducing the first DRAG fragment, slowing the phrase “nothing was corrected” until each word occupies nearly a full bar. Jelena plays isolated upper Rhodes octaves between the stretched words. Andre remains silent for the first half of the track. Luka enters beneath the longest silence with a low chalumeau tone. The first sub note appears only after the final word of the DRAG phrase has fully ended. In the closing minute, Milena repeats the same sentence live at normal speed, calmly and without emphasis. The tape does not return. The record ends on the live bow release and room tone.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Typed transcript page with one obvious date discrepancy left uncrossed, no red correction marks, a cassette time counter frozen mid-number, one thin bowed-string waveform beside a massively stretched speech waveform. Lyric fragments limited to documentary language around dates, signatures, routing, and the unchanged line “nothing was corrected.”

